#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "99f2f8a2-f5aa-5a32-b29f-8e1f79f504cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21692" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - 2y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 4x(t) + y(t) - 6$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T$. What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - 2v(t); v^{′}(t) = 4u(t) + v(t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e61881ec-4361-554d-b374-35b8b3f64366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21693" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T$. What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad y(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad x'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad v'(t) = y'(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n$x^{′}(t)|= x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; u^{′}(t)|= u^{2}(t) + u(t) + (v(t) - 2) + 2; = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t)$.\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10; v^{′}(t)|= 5u(t) - (v(t) - 2)^{2} + 3(v(t) - 2) + 10; = 5u(t) - (v^{2}(t) - 4v(t) + 4) + 3v(t) - 6 + 10; = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/id #uuid "9215f93a-14ad-5be5-9f27-25ba556252e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21694" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n1. $y=3x+3$\n2. $y=-x$\n3. $y=3x$\n4. $y=-3x$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00257d2-50ff-5578-a35c-672bf5ed8825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = 2u(t) + v^{2}(t); v^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) - 3v(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220436} {:answer/id #uuid "a79d76d6-ccf4-5413-b5df-1761283b5b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + v^{2}(t); v^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - v(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220437} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed0b827-10e6-5e1c-9d68-7cc8299fc71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - v^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220438} {:answer/id #uuid "282c3f41-9b26-51cb-a39f-eb4ba7dfad3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = 2u^{2}(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = u(t) - 3v^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220439} {:answer/id #uuid "769e9e3e-d659-5f92-92ff-488b42b6c337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = u(t) + v^{2}(t); v^{′}(t) = 3u^{2}(t) - v(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed0b827-10e6-5e1c-9d68-7cc8299fc71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - v^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220438} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1fada6-d0c2-56b3-bd12-2308b58afaae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220435}] :question/id #uuid "6a3c0b53-eab9-5b58-92da-6bae03210acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333671" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) - 2x(t) + y(t) - 1; y^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 4y(t) - 7$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1, 2]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f738e6-1fb4-50ef-bba1-09ba7a648fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220448} {:answer/id #uuid "f633c220-ef07-55cd-a8fe-4fecf9903200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220449} {:answer/id #uuid "1133f9ec-29d6-5b33-bb14-ce70b1fe75c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220450} {:answer/id #uuid "2876d161-685b-561d-8c20-e82d1bcc43d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220451} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd4197-451c-59c1-827e-4e9b80259440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f738e6-1fb4-50ef-bba1-09ba7a648fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220448} :answer-field/id #uuid "53c6b181-ec24-5a98-bbac-4b80559e73f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220447}] :question/id #uuid "cc805b5f-d7a6-53b7-9121-090baedc8eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333683" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $x^{∗}$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y = 2x - 3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u = x - x^{∗}$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?"}]}
